Let $f(x,y)$ be a smooth real-valued function defined in a neighbourhood of the origin $(0,0)\in\mathbb{R}^2$. Denote by $f_x$ and $f_y$ its partial derivatives. Consider the rational expression
$$ F(x,y) \;=\; \frac{f_x\,(x - f_x) \;-\; f\,f_y}{\,\text{(an appropriate denominator vanishing at }0)\,}, $$
or, in the form arising directly from the problem, the function
$$ G(x,y) \;=\; f_x(x - f_x) - f\,f_y. $$
Describe explicitly all smooth functions $f(x,y)$ defined near $(0,0)$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f_y \not\equiv 0$ in any neighbourhood of $(0,0)$ (i.e. $f_y$ is not identically zero);
\item $f_y(0,0) = 0$;
\item the expression $f_x(x - f_x) - f f_y$, viewed as a function defined where the natural denominator is nonzero, extends to a function that is locally smooth in a full neighbourhood of $(0,0)$.
\end{enumerate}
Give a complete local classification (up to smooth coordinate changes preserving the relevant structure) of all such germs $f$.

